arm64: idle polling only while it pays

Patch 0011 grows the poll window whenever a CPU is woken soon after it
gave up polling, on the reasoning that a longer window would have caught
that wakeup. Under a trickle of wakeups that reasoning runs away: Frigate
on one camera woke each vCPU about 300 times a second, most polls timed
out, the short sleep after each grew the window back, and polling was 60
to 80 ms of every second across four vCPUs, a third of the VMM's CPU for
the whole stack (20.7% of a core against 13.9% without, on 16 vCPUs).

So only a poll that another CPU ended grows the window (an interrupt
reaches a CPU in WFI as fast, so a poll one ended bought nothing), a poll
that timed out halves it, and every `idle.poll_epoch` polls (16) each CPU
weighs what it spent spinning against the wakeups it caught: more than
`idle.poll_cost_ns` (20 us) a catch, or none at all, and it stops polling
for a backoff doubling from 10 ms to `idle.poll_backoff_max_ns` (250 ms).
A thread pool's handoffs pay every epoch and never back off: the
cross-vCPU pipe round trip stays at 2.2 us (8.5 us without polling), and
comes back within about a fifth of a second from a backed-off CPU.

While a container's stream to an accelerator has moved a message in the
last millisecond (0011's traffic signal), a poll an interrupt ended is a
catch too, a timeout does not shrink the window, and the backoff does not
apply: the reply a token loop waits for arrives as an interrupt, and a CPU
already awake takes it without the host waking a parked thread first.
Without this, llama.cpp's generation over Metal RPC read 267-273 t/s on
the M5 against 292-294.

`idle.polled` reports, per CPU, how many polls timed out, were ended by
an interrupt, or by another CPU, and the nanoseconds each kind spent.
