+/*
+ * Whether polling pays. A poll another CPU ends saves that CPU an IPI and
+ * this one a trip out of WFI, a few microseconds of the host's time; a poll
+ * that times out saves nothing, nor does one an interrupt ends, since the
+ * interrupt reaches a CPU in WFI as fast. Under a trickle of wakeups (a home
+ * server's camera frames and its Python threads) most polls time out, and
+ * the window grown by each short sleep after them was a third of the VMM's
+ * CPU. So only a poll another CPU ended grows the window, a poll that timed
+ * out halves it, and every poll_epoch polls the CPU weighs the spinning
+ * against the wakeups it caught: more than poll_cost_ns a catch, or none,
+ * and it stops polling for a backoff that doubles from 10 ms to
+ * poll_backoff_max_ns while polling keeps not paying. A thread pool's
+ * handoffs, which polling exists for, pay every epoch and never back off.
+ */
